"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[33395],{633395:e=>{e.exports='# Data Science: ML Workflows and Model Serving\n\nDomain knowledge for machine learning model development, experiment tracking, and model serving on Databricks.\n\n## ML Development Workflow\n\nFollow this systematic approach for ML tasks:\n\n1. **Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** — Understand data through profiling, visualizations, and statistics\n2. **Data Preprocessing** — Handle missing values, encode categoricals, split data\n3. **Model Selection & Training** — Choose models based on task type and data characteristics\n4. **Hyperparameter Tuning** — Optimize with systematic parameter search (prefer `optuna`)\n5. **Evaluation** — Validate with appropriate metrics\n6. **Experiment Tracking** — Log everything with MLflow (mandatory)\n\n## EDA Best Practices\n\n- Call `describe()`, `shape()`, `head()`, and `info()` on the DataFrame\n- Create a correlogram of features if applicable\n- Create at least two visualizations of correlations between features and target\n- For classification: visualize class frequencies\n- For time series: visualize a portion of the data over time\n- Create a feature type inventory table (numeric, categorical, ordinal, etc.)\n\n## Data Preprocessing\n\n- **Missing values**: Impute numerics with mean using `SimpleImputer`; `dropna` as last resort\n- **Categorical encoding**: One-hot encode low-cardinality categoricals; use `category_encoders` library\n- **Data splitting**: Train/validation/test split; training set should be largest\n\n## Model Selection by Task\n\n### Classification\n\n- **Linear separability check**: Train LinearSVC with high C — if 100% accuracy, data is linearly separable\n- **Small-medium data**: Decision trees, random forests (interpretable), gradient-boosted trees (accuracy)\n- **Large data**: XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost with early stopping\n- **Deep learning**: PyTorch sigmoid/softmax classifiers for large complex datasets\n\n### Regression\n\n- **Linear**: Spark MLlib LinearRegression, sklearn Ridge/Lasso, PyTorch Linear\n- **Non-linear**: PolynomialFeatures + LinearRegression (small data), deep neural networks (large data)\n- Always compute and verify residuals are zero-mean normal after training\n\n### Evaluation Metrics\n\n- **Classification**: Accuracy, precision/recall, F1, ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, confusion matrix\n- **Regression**: Residual analysis, MSE, R-squared\n- **Multi-label**: Subset accuracy, Hamming loss, per-label ROC-AUC\n\n## MLflow Experiment Tracking\n\nAlways log: hyperparameters, metrics, model artifacts, signatures, training duration, random seeds.\n\n```python\nimport mlflow\n\nmlflow.set_registry_uri("databricks-uc")\n\nwith mlflow.start_run():\n    model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n    mlflow.log_metric("accuracy", accuracy)\n    signature = mlflow.models.infer_signature(X_train, model.predict(X_train))\n    mlflow.sklearn.log_model(\n        model, name="model",\n        signature=signature,\n        input_example=X_train[:5],\n        registered_model_name="catalog.schema.my_model",\n    )\n```\n\nUse `mlflow.sklearn.autolog()` (or `.xgboost`, `.lightgbm`, `.pytorch`, etc.) for automatic logging.\n\nNote: `name=` in `log_model(...)` is the MLflow 3.x keyword. On MLflow 2.x runtimes, use `artifact_path=` instead.\n\n## Model Serving\n\nLog models to Unity Catalog and deploy to serving endpoints.\n\n**Critical**: Always include both `signature` and `input_example` when logging models.\n\n### HuggingFace Transformers\n\nAlways wrap in a `pipeline()` before logging:\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import pipeline\nclassifier = pipeline("text-classification", model="distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english")\nmlflow.transformers.log_model(transformers_model=classifier, ...)\n```\n\n### Custom PyFunc Models\n\n```python\nclass MyModel(mlflow.pyfunc.PythonModel):\n    def load_context(self, context):\n        self.model = load_from_artifacts(context.artifacts)\n    def predict(self, context, model_input):\n        return self.model.predict(model_input)\n\nmlflow.pyfunc.log_model(python_model=MyModel(), artifacts={...}, signature=sig, ...)\n```\n\n### Pre-Deployment Validation\n\n```python\nmlflow.models.predict(model_uri=model_info.model_uri, input_data=sample, env_manager="uv")\n```\n\n## How to Work with ML via the Databricks Agent\n\n- **Run ML code**: Use `executeCode` to execute Python/SQL on attached compute\n- **Create ML notebooks**: `createAsset({ assetType: \'notebook\', name: \'ML Analysis\' })` then `editAsset` with code cells\n- **Explore tables**: Use `readTable` and `tableSearch` to understand source data schemas\n- **Find models**: `runDatabricksCli` with `unity-catalog models list --catalog-name <cat> --schema-name <schema> --output JSON`\n- **Check serving endpoints**: `runDatabricksCli` with `serving-endpoints list --output JSON`\n- **Navigate to notebook**: `openAsset({ assetType: \'notebook\', assetId: \'<id>\' })` for continued work\n'}}]);
